<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#salinisation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salinisation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salinisation is the build-up of salts in soil, often caused by long-term irrigation in hot, dry climates where evaporation leaves mineral deposits behind. It reduces crop yields and can make land unusable for farming. Possible salinisation of agricultural land in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> may have reduced productivity and strained the empire's economic base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salinisation.jpg" alt="Salinisation" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USDA Employee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salinity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Soil salinity is the salt content of the soil, and salinisation is the process by which it increases. Salts occur naturally in soils and water, and the ions responsible are sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium and chloride. Where rainfall or irrigation is sufficient, salts released as soil minerals weather are flushed out by drainage water. Under intense evaporation in dry periods they may instead rise through capillarity and collect near the surface, which leads to naturally saline soils in dry regions, as in large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Salinization can arise from natural processes such as mineral weathering or the gradual withdrawal of an ocean, and from artificial ones such as irrigation and road salt. Almost all water, even natural rainfall, contains dissolved salts. When plants use irrigation water the salts are left behind in the root zone and begin to accumulate, and the problem is much greater where drainage is poor or saline water is used.</p><p>Disrupting natural drainage can also cause it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> was built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1970, the continuous high level of the water table led to the salinisation of arable land. Clearing trees for farming has the same effect in some dry regions, since deep tree roots are replaced by the shallow roots of annual crops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Salinity harms plant growth and yield, damages roads, bricks and pipes, lowers water quality and can in the end cause erosion when vegetation cover collapses. It is an important land degradation problem. Sensitive crops lose vigour even in slightly saline soils, while only salt-resistant crops thrive in severely saline ones. Salinity can be reduced by leaching salts out with good-quality irrigation water, together with control of the water table and drainage, and gypsum is the most common compound used to reclaim sodic soil, where sodium predominates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soil salinity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Environmental factors, including possible <b>salinisation</b> of farmland, may have reduced productivity, and surviving correspondence records grain shortages and soaring prices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
